<p>President Trump has ordered all US federal agencies to immediately stop using AI technology from San Francisco-based Anthropic, escalating a weeks-long dispute between the company and the Pentagon over how the military can use Anthropic’s Claude models. The Pentagon has also moved to label Anthropic a “supply-chain risk,” a step that could restrict government contracting and widen the impact beyond existing Defense Department deployments. Anthropic says it set firm limits on uses it sees as incompatible with its policies—especially mass domestic surveillance and fully autonomous weapons—while also noting its tools are already used by defense and intelligence agencies for national-security work. The Pentagon says it operates within the law and that suppliers should not set operational terms; the administration has set a transition period for agencies currently using Anthropic products.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/lrV_nDhKuc3XOyCAwXUJY2iA6PaJWQUseQopmTPKtw__iwQPKMhK4_lb4515H5hiKgc9YANnhHE9awBOX7EH-nPFN-9OE2G2xDzS5QcsN5jq' alt='Illustration photo showing the Anthropic logo (file image).' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Legal showdown: Anthropic says it will challenge the Pentagon’s “supply-chain risk” designation in court, calling it legally unsound and a risky precedent for companies negotiating with the government.</li><li>Pressure tools: Reporting says the Pentagon threatened to compel cooperation under the Defense Production Act if Anthropic did not accept unconditional military-use terms by the deadline.</li><li>National security framing: Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth accused Anthropic of seeking “veto power” over military operations and said the department must keep access to the models for any lawful defense purpose.</li><li>Tech industry ripple: Semafor reported that parts of Silicon Valley publicly backed Anthropic, and a small group of Google employees urged their company to take a similar stance on military-related AI use.</li><li>Competing vendor move: OpenAI publicized a new Pentagon partnership soon after the Anthropic decision, with CEO Sam Altman highlighting the agreement as OpenAI expands its government work.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Anthropic (Dario Amodei): CEO Dario Amodei said the company could not “in good conscience” agree to unrestricted military use. He argued that mass domestic surveillance clashes with democratic values, and that fully autonomous weapons are still too unreliable without human control. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976334/anthropic-refuses-to-allow-pentagon-unrestricted-use-of-artificial-intelligence'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Trump administration: President Trump said he directed every federal agency to immediately stop using Anthropic technology and warned of “major civil and criminal consequences” if the company did not cooperate with the government’s transition off its products. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976371/trump-directs-us-agencies-to-toss-anthropics-ai-as-pentagon-calls-startup-a-supply-risk'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>US Defense Department: A senior Pentagon official pushed back on Anthropic’s concerns, saying the department acts within the law and that legality governs its activities. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976334/anthropic-refuses-to-allow-pentagon-unrestricted-use-of-artificial-intelligence'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>OpenAI: OpenAI said it struck a deal for the Pentagon to use its models on the department’s classified network, with safeguards. The company said it would keep safety guardrails in place. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/sam-altmans-openai-signs-deal-with-pentagon-hours-after-trump-slammed-anthropic-11147907#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>World Socialist Web Site: WSWS framed the dispute as a fight between competing elite factions over how to integrate AI into the US military, rather than a simple ethics-versus-militarism clash. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/02/28/ildx-f28.html'>WSWS</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/why-the-trump-administration-is-clashing-with-ai-firm-anthropic'>Why the Trump administration is clashing with AI firm Anthropic</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/02/28/ildx-f28.html'>Trump blacklists Anthropic, orders all federal agencies to cease use of AI firm’s technology</a> - wsws.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3344946/trump-tells-us-government-immediately-stop-using-anthropic-ai-tech'>Anthropic vows to sue US after Trump’s call to stop using firm’s AI, OpenAI reaches deal</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/sam-altmans-openai-signs-deal-with-pentagon-hours-after-trump-slammed-anthropic-11147907#publisher=newsstand'>Sam Altman&#x27;s OpenAI Signs Deal With Pentagon Amid Trump vs Anthropic</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/1aeff07f-6221-4577-b19c-887bb654c585'>Anthropic to sue Trump administration after AI lab is labelled security risk</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/27/trump-anthropic-ai-federal-agencies'>Trump orders US agencies to stop use of Anthropic technology amid dispute over ethics of AI</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/openai-gives-pentagon-ai-model-access-after-anthropic-dust-up'>OpenAI gives Pentagon AI model access after Anthropic dust up</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976371/trump-directs-us-agencies-to-toss-anthropics-ai-as-pentagon-calls-startup-a-supply-risk'>Trump directs US agencies to toss Anthropic’s AI as Pentagon calls startup a supply risk</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/27/trump-orders-federal-agencies-to-stop-using-anthropic-as-dispute-escalates'>Trump orders federal agencies to stop using Anthropic as dispute escalates</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a h